# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# Compression layer: Section 4 (Literature Review)

REVS = {
    "P98": ("This is an integrative review rather than a systematic review, and the distinction is deliberate.",
            "This is an integrative review rather than a systematic review, and the distinction is deliberate. A systematic review answers a narrow question by exhausting a defined body of evidence; an integrative review synthesises literature from several traditions in order to frame a problem and identify a gap (Torraco, 2016). The present question sits between fields that do not share a vocabulary \u2014 energy engineering, energy justice, Living Lab research and posthumanist philosophy each describe part of the problem, and none describes all of it."),
    "P99": ("The review follows a documented search strategy, set out in Table 2.1.",
            "The review follows a documented search strategy, set out in Table 2.1. It does not claim to be exhaustive and does not use the language of PRISMA; its claim is to be integrative and critical. If examiners later require a systematic review, a protocol can be registered and a second review conducted."),
    "P102": ("The review is organised around six bodies of work:",
             "The review is organised around six bodies of work: energy poverty and energy security; just transition and energy justice; Living Labs and systems thinking; participation and acceptance; decentralised technology, with particular attention to biogas; and posthumanism as applied to energy and design. Each section closes by identifying what the literature contributes and what it leaves unresolved."),
    "P104": ("Energy poverty is usually defined as the inability",
             "Energy poverty is usually defined as the inability to obtain the energy services needed for a decent life \u2014 a definition deliberately broader than \u201clack of electricity\u201d, covering quantity and quality, affordability, reliability, safety and the time required to obtain energy. A household with a connection it cannot afford to use is energy poor; so is a household that cooks on paraffin because the supply fails in the evening."),
    "P105": ("South Africa's energy poverty is historically produced.",
             "South Africa's energy poverty is historically produced. Apartheid spatial planning located black households far from work and services, and the electricity system was built to serve industry and a minority of households. The post-1994 electrification programme raised the connection rate from around a third of households to around nine in ten (Statistics South Africa, 2023), an achievement that nonetheless left energy poverty intact: tariffs rose sharply, dwellings are often poorly insulated and inadequately wired, and interruptions became routine. Formal access coexists with informal practice \u2014 households are connected and still stack fuels, borrow electricity and cook on paraffin when the power fails."),
    "P106": ("Figure 2.1 represents that pattern as a chain from carrier to service,",
             "Figure 2.1 represents that pattern as a chain from carrier to service, with the losses at each step: the right-hand column is where a technical account usually stops, and the bottom band is the part it omits."),
    "P109": ("The South African literature has documented this pattern in detail.",
             "The South African literature has documented this pattern in detail. Musango (2022) uses quantitative storytelling in the Groenheuwel settlement to show how gender, income and dwelling characteristics interact in household energy transitions. Musango et al. (2020) argue that the security of energy services, rather than of supply, is the appropriate organising concept for poor urban environments, and Batinge et al. (2022) formalise the insight in a systems model linking gendered innovation and service security through feedback. Anditi et al. (2022) provide a gender analysis framework that makes visible the different roles, burdens and knowledge of women and men, and Oosthuizen et al. (2020) identify, in a structured review, the factors that have hindered the mainstreaming of gendered energy interventions."),
    "P110": ("Three limitations recur.",
             "Three limitations recur. First, households are treated as homogeneous units, yet a household is a set of relationships in which age, gender, tenure and income shape who decides, who cooks, who pays and who bears the risk \u2014 a technology acceptable to the household head may be unacceptable to the person who has to clean it. Second, the urban settlement is sometimes conflated with the rural village, although the two differ in density, land markets, tenure, infrastructure and proximity to services. Third, the literature is stronger on description than on design: it explains why energy poverty persists but offers little guidance on what an engineer should change in a prototype, a tariff or a service model."),
    "P111": ("Energy security, in this literature, is best understood",
             "Energy security, in this literature, is best understood as a household-level accomplishment: security means obtaining the needed services across normal and disrupted conditions without unacceptable cost or risk. The definition connects the national conversation about generation capacity to the daily conversation about cooking, and it makes the distribution of security an ethical question, because security for one household can be purchased at the expense of another \u2014 a landlord's prepaid meter secures revenue and transfers the risk of arrears to the tenant."),
    "P113": ("The idea of a just transition began in the labour",
             "The idea of a just transition began in the labour and environmental movements of the 1980s and 1990s, which insisted that workers in polluting industries should not bear the cost of environmental protection. In its contemporary form, a just transition is fair in its outcomes, open in its procedures and respectful of the identities and knowledge of affected communities (Newell and Mulvaney, 2013; Swilling and Annecke, 2012). Winkler (2020) gives the concept a neo-Gramscian reading: transitions are shaped by the balance of social forces, and a transition that does not shift that balance will reproduce existing inequalities under a greener label."),
    "P114": ("In South Africa, the just transition has become official policy.",
             "In South Africa, the just transition has become official policy. The Presidential Climate Commission's framework sets out principles for distribution, procedure and restoration, and the Just Energy Transition Partnership has attached international finance to the coal phase-down (Presidential Climate Commission, 2022). Implementation research is less optimistic: Mirzania et al. (2023) identify structural and institutional barriers to powering past coal, including municipal capacity, vested interests and the social dependency of coal towns, and Molepo et al. (2025) find that the barriers are distributed across policy, finance, skills and governance and that they interact. The transition is not blocked by a single missing factor."),
    "P115": ("Energy justice supplies the normative vocabulary",
             "Energy justice supplies the normative vocabulary for evaluating these processes. The most widely used formulation identifies three tenets \u2014 distributive, procedural and recognition justice (McCauley et al., 2013; Jenkins et al., 2016). Sovacool and Dworkin (2015) add principles including availability, affordability, due process and intergenerational equity; Heffron and McCauley (2017) add restorative justice, the repair of past harm; and Walker and Day (2012) argue, for fuel poverty, that a failure of recognition is itself a form of distributive injustice rather than a separate dimension to be traded off."),
    "P116": ("The literature has two weaknesses for present purposes.",
             "The literature has two weaknesses for present purposes. The first is scale: energy justice is usually applied to policy, siting conflicts and large infrastructure, and its principles do not tell an engineer which material to specify or how to organise maintenance. The second is operationalisation: recognition is often reduced to \u201cstakeholder engagement\u201d and procedure to \u201cconsultation\u201d, and both reductions lose the critical content of the concepts \u2014 a consultation can be procedurally correct and still legitimise a decision taken elsewhere."),
    "P117": ("What the study takes from this literature is a set of evaluative questions,",
             "What the study takes from this literature is a set of evaluative questions, not a ready-made design method: who receives what, through which procedure, with whose knowledge recognised, and with what repair for past harm? Section 6 turns these questions into indicators; Section 5 explains why they cannot be answered by a purely technical assessment."),
    "P119": ("A Living Lab is a real-world arrangement for innovation",
             "A Living Lab is a real-world arrangement for innovation in which users, researchers, firms and public bodies collaborate over time. Most definitions agree on four elements: a real-life setting, multiple stakeholders, co-creation with users, and iterative experimentation (Bergvall-K\u00e5reborn and St\u00e5hlbr\u00f6st, 2009; Leminen et al., 2012). Labs have been classified by who drives them, by sector and by their physical or virtual constitution, but Hossain et al. (2019), in a systematic review, find the literature fragmented, with many studies describing individual labs without comparing them. Dell'Era and Landoni (2014) position the Living Lab between user-centred and participatory design, making clear that it is an organisational form rather than a consultation method."),
    "P120": ("The energy Living Lab literature is smaller.",
             "The energy Living Lab literature is smaller. Mbatha and Musango (2022) find the concept used inconsistently \u2014 as a space, a method or a network \u2014 and propose a six-phase process for setting up an energy Living Lab in an urban informal settlement. Mukama et al. (2022) describe Living Labs for gendered energy innovation in poor urban environments, Petrulaityte et al. (2022) present the GENS co-design toolkit, and von Wirth et al. (2019) show that urban labs influence sustainability transitions through embedding, learning and network formation, but that their impact depends on how the lab is connected to the wider regime."),
    "P121": ("Systems thinking supplies a different set of resources.",
             "Systems thinking supplies a different set of resources. Checkland (1981) argues that systems are ways of organising inquiry, so that a system's boundary is a choice with political consequences. Meadows (2008) shows how feedback, stocks and flows produce behaviour that cannot be inferred from the parts. Midgley (2000) develops boundary critique as a central move, and Sterman (2000) provides the vocabulary of causal loops and delays. Cilliers (1998) cautions that complex systems cannot be fully represented, so any model is a reduction to be used with care."),
    "P122": ("The combination of Living Lab and systems thinking is often asserted",
             "The combination of Living Lab and systems thinking is often asserted rather than derived. A \u201csystemic Living Lab\u201d is usually a lab that uses systems tools, not one whose boundaries, stakeholder set, feedback architecture and evaluation criteria are derived from systems theory. That distinction is the subject of Section 5.8, and it matters because a lab that is systemic only in vocabulary will reproduce the conventional boundary of the project, with the wider settlement treated as context."),
    "P123": ("The empirical evidence on energy Living Labs in African informal settlements is thin:",
             "The empirical evidence on energy Living Labs in African informal settlements is thin: the South African work is recent and mostly descriptive, and there are few longitudinal, comparative or counterfactual evaluations. This is a statement about the state of the evidence rather than about the researchers, and it shapes the claims this study can make: it can contribute a carefully documented case, but it cannot establish general effectiveness."),
    "P125": ("Participation has a long and sceptical literature.",
             "Participation has a long and sceptical literature. Arnstein's (1969) ladder distinguishes manipulation and therapy from consultation, partnership and citizen control, and its central insight survives: the difference between consultation and control is the difference between legitimising a decision and sharing it. Sanders and Stappers (2008) describe the shift from designing for users to designing with them, and von Hippel (2005) shows that users can be important sources of innovation when their needs are unusual and their knowledge tacit."),
    "P126": ("Energy research has adopted participation as a norm",
             "Energy research has adopted participation as a norm while examining its conditions more slowly. W\u00fcstenhagen et al. (2007) distinguish social acceptance at three levels \u2014 socio-political, community and market \u2014 showing that a technology can be acceptable in principle and resisted in a particular place; but the model remains attitudinal, asking what people think rather than what the technology, the infrastructure and household practice do together."),
    "P127": ("The dominant model of acceptance in engineering and information systems",
             "The dominant model of acceptance in engineering and information systems is the Technology Acceptance Model, in which perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use predict intention and use (Davis, 1989), extended by UTAUT with social influence and facilitating conditions (Venkatesh et al., 2003). Both models have been enormously productive, and both assume an individual chooser, a finished artefact, a measurable intention and a context that can be treated as background. They have little to say about the cost of fuel, the reliability of supply, the division of labour, the terms of tenure or the material properties of a dwelling. A TAM study can report that residents find a biogas system useful; it cannot explain why the system is abandoned when the water supply fails."),
    "P128": ("Practice theory and the energy cultures framework offer an alternative.",
             "Practice theory and the energy cultures framework offer an alternative. Shove (2003) shows that consumption is shaped by collective, material conventions of comfort, cleanliness and convenience, and Stephenson et al. (2010) propose that energy behaviour emerges from the interaction of norms, practices and material culture. These approaches treat technology and practice as co-constitutive, but they are stronger on explanation than on design."),
    "P129": ("Participation also has failure modes that the literature documents",
             "Participation also has failure modes the literature documents and projects ignore: consultation can be tokenistic, meetings dominated by those with time and status, expectations raised and disappointed, benefits captured by elites, and communities fatigued by repeated participation in projects that do not endure. These are not arguments against participation but for designing it with the same care as the technology, and for evaluating it rather than assuming its virtue."),
    "P131": ("Anaerobic digestion is a mature technology:",
             "Anaerobic digestion is a mature technology: bacteria break down organic matter without oxygen, producing a gas that is roughly 50 to 70 per cent methane and a residue usable as a soil amendment. The process needs a consistent feedstock, water, a temperature range that supports microbial activity, and an adequate retention time. In practice, designing a digester means designing a supply chain and a maintenance arrangement as much as a vessel."),
    "P132": ("Biogas has been promoted across Africa for decades, with mixed results.",
             "Biogas has been promoted across Africa for decades with mixed results. Successful installations share a reliable feedstock supply, a user who understands the system, a maintenance arrangement with access to parts, and a use for the gas that fits existing cooking practice; failures share the opposite. The technical literature calls these \u201cnon-technical barriers\u201d. A posthumanist reading treats them as part of the technology: the digester is the vessel plus the feedstock, the water, the labour, the stove, the spare parts and the agreement about who maintains it."),
    "P135": ("Posthumanism has been applied extensively in education,",
             "Posthumanism has been applied extensively in education, geography, feminist technoscience and design, but rarely in energy engineering. Forlano (2017) argues that taking non-human agency seriously changes what counts as a design problem. Ulmer (2017) sets out posthumanism as a research methodology. Aboalez (2025) makes the case for a posthumanist social approach to energy transition in engineering education \u2014 the closest precedent for this study, discussed in Section 5.4."),
    "P136": ("The gap is not that posthumanism has never been mentioned in energy research.",
             "The gap is not that posthumanism has never been mentioned in energy research, but that there is no developed account of how distributed agency shapes the design, acceptance and use of energy technologies in informal settlements. Energy justice says what justice requires, Living Lab research says how to organise collaboration, and posthumanism says how to think about human\u2013technology relations. What is missing is a framework that connects the three at the scale of a settlement project."),
    "P143": ("The study is positioned at the intersection of these gaps,",
             "The study is positioned at the intersection of these gaps, not at the centre of any one of them. It cannot claim to advance energy economics, posthumanist philosophy or Living Lab methodology as such; it can show how a small number of concepts from those fields assemble into a framework useful for a particular class of problem, and test that framework against a case. Two implications sometimes carried by this literature are treated here as hypotheses rather than premises: that participation produces acceptance, and that acceptance produces justice."),
    "P144": ("The gap statement also sets the boundaries of what is not claimed.",
             "The gap statement also sets the limits of what is not claimed: the reviewed literature contains few accounts that combine Living Lab and systems approaches to energy poverty, and none that combines them with a posthumanist ontology. The claim made here is correspondingly narrow \u2014 about the reviewed literature, not about all studies ever conducted. That narrowing is recorded, with the other corrections, in Appendix A."),
}
